<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paulette Nardal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1985">1985</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paulette Nardal was a French writer and journalist from Martinique and one of the drivers of the development of black literary consciousness and of the creation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negritude">Négritude</a> genre. In 1920 she was the first black person to study at the Sorbonne, and with her sisters held a salon that brought together black intellectuals from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>; her translations introduced French intellectuals to writers of the Harlem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. In 1931 she co-founded La Revue du Monde Noir. Injured when her ship was torpedoed in 1939, she returned to Martinique, founded Le Rassemblement féminin in 1944 and later worked at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paulette-nardal.jpg" alt="Paulette Nardal" width="200" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paulette_Nardal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
